The deadlock occurs because both processes try to access the same two resources in a different order. Process P1 first locks R1 and then waits to lock R2. 
At the same time, Process P2 locks R2 and waits to lock R1. Since each process is waiting for the resource held by the other, neither of them can continue.
This creates a circular waiting condition, which is one of the four necessary conditions for deadlock.
The exact reason for the deadlock is:
P1 holds R1 and waits for R2.
P2 holds R2 and waits for R1.
Neither process releases its first resource.
Both remain blocked forever.To eliminate the deadlock without changing the process logic, the resources should always be locked in the same order. 
For example, both processes should first lock R1 and then R2. This removes the circular wait condition. Another possible optimization is to use a
timeout while waiting for a resource. If a process cannot obtain the second resource within the specified time, it releases the first resource and retries later. 
This prevents the system from remaining in a deadlock state.

