The CPU utilization is only 30%, even though processes are ready to execute. The scheduler log shows repeated transitions from 
Ready → Waiting → Ready, indicating that the processes are spending most of their time waiting for I/O operations instead of performing CPU execution.The main
reason for low CPU utilization is frequent I/O requests. Whenever a process requests an I/O operation, it moves to the waiting state. 
Since many processes behave in the same way, the CPU remains idle for a significant amount of time.Another reason may be poor scheduling, where the 
operating system fails to schedule another ready process immediately after one process enters the waiting state. This results in unnecessary idle CPU time.
The following system-level optimizations can improve CPU utilization:
Increase the degree of multiprogramming so that more processes are available in memory.
Use efficient CPU scheduling algorithms to switch quickly to another ready process.
Improve I/O scheduling to reduce waiting time.
Use asynchronous I/O whenever possible so that CPU and I/O operations can occur simultaneously.
These improvements ensure that whenever one process waits for I/O, another process can use the CPU, thereby increasing overall utilization.

