The program experiences frequent page faults because it uses a large amount of memory. The heap occupies 150 MB, and each of the 25 threads 
has its own stack of 1 MB. Since many threads access memory simultaneously, the operating system frequently loads pages from secondary storage into RAM, 
increasing page faults.Although the system has 2 GB of RAM, poor memory management and unnecessary memory allocation can still cause performance degradation.
The first optimization is to reduce the number of active threads or decrease the stack size allocated to each thread. This reduces memory consumption and lowers 
the number of page faults.The second optimization is to improve heap memory management. The program should free unused memory immediately and avoid 
creating unnecessary objects. Efficient memory allocation reduces memory fragmentation and improves performance.
Additional improvements include:
Using thread pools instead of creating many threads.
Improving data locality.
Using efficient page replacement algorithms.
These optimizations reduce page faults, improve memory utilization, and increase the overall speed of the application.

