The process P5 is placed in Queue 3, which follows the FCFS scheduling policy. Since Queue 1 and Queue 2 always have higher priority, they continue to
receive CPU time whenever new processes arrive. As a result, P5 keeps waiting indefinitely.This problem is called starvation. Starvation occurs when a 
low-priority process does not receive CPU time because higher-priority processes continuously execute.The root cause is the fixed priority scheduling
mechanism used by the multilevel queue scheduler. There is no mechanism to promote waiting processes to a higher priority.The best optimization is aging.
In aging, the priority of a process increases gradually as its waiting time increases. After waiting for a certain period, P5 is promoted to a higher-priority 
queue, allowing it to execute.
Other improvements include:
Allocating a fixed percentage of CPU time to lower-priority queues.
Using a Multilevel Feedback Queue scheduler.
Periodically boosting the priority of long-waiting processes.
These techniques ensure fairness and prevent starvation while maintaining good system performance.

5.
The server performs about 20,000 context switches every second, creating a large amount of overhead. The main reason is that the scheduler uses a 1 ms 
time quantum, while the average CPU burst time of a thread is 2 ms. Most threads are interrupted before completing their CPU burst, causing frequent context 
switches.Another reason is the very high frequency of I/O operations. Threads often move between the running and waiting states, increasing the number of 
scheduler decisions and context switches.Frequent context switching wastes CPU time because the operating system must save the current process state,
load the next process state, update scheduling information, and refresh CPU registers. This overhead reduces useful CPU execution.
The first optimization is to increase the time quantum. For example, increasing it from 1 ms to 4 ms or 5 ms allows threads to complete more work
before being preempted, reducing unnecessary switches.The second optimization is to improve I/O scheduling. Buffering requests, batching I/O operations, 
and using asynchronous I/O reduce the number of waiting transitions and context switches.
Other possible improvements are:
Using thread pools.
Reducing unnecessary preemption.
Selecting an appropriate scheduling algorithm based on workload.
